course: books
lesson: 66
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XI, part 2: «Like Summer Tempests Came His Tears» (how Toad Hall was taken, a gun at the gate and a sunken boat)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The Rat tells the whole story. While Toad was in prison, the river-bankers stood up for him, but the Wild Wood animals said it served him right and he would never come back. Mole and Badger were sure he would, somehow, so they moved into Toad Hall to keep it aired and ready for him.
explain: Then, one very dark, wet, windy night, the Wild Wooders attacked from three sides at once: weasels up the front drive, ferrets through the kitchen-garden, stoats into the conservatory and billiard-room. Mole and Badger were sitting by the fire, unarmed and taken by surprise; there were hundreds against two. They were beaten with sticks and pushed out into the cold and the rain. Ever since, the weasels have been living in Toad Hall, lying in bed half the day, eating Toad's food and singing rude songs about him.
explain: Toad grabs a stick and marches off to see about it. At his gate a long yellow ferret with a gun calls «Who comes there?» and fires; Toad drops flat, the shot whistles over his head, and he runs home to the sound of thin little laughs.
explain: Next he rows the Rat's boat up the river to the garden front. As he passes under the little bridge — Crash! — a great stone dropped by two stoats smashes through the bottom of the boat, and Toad has to swim for it while they laugh. The Rat is very cross: his boat is lost and his nice suit ruined. Toad apologises fully and promises to do nothing without the Rat's advice; the Rat says they must wait for Mole and Badger.
fact: The river-bankers said Toad «had been infamously treated» (very unfairly); the Wild Wood animals said «served you right» and that he was «done for this time».
fact: Mole and Badger «argued from history»: no law had ever beaten «cheek and plausibility such as yours, combined with the power of a long purse» (plausibility = sounding believable; a long purse = lots of money).
fact: So Mole and Badger moved into Toad Hall «and sleep there, and keep it aired, and have it all ready for you when you turned up».
fact: The attack came on «a very dark night, and blowing hard, too, and raining simply cats and dogs» (raining cats and dogs = raining very hard).
fact: Weasels crept up the carriage-drive to the front door; ferrets took the backyard through the kitchen-garden; stoats held the conservatory, the billiard-room and the French windows onto the lawn.
fact: Mole and Badger were telling stories by the fire in the smoking-room; «They were unarmed, and taken by surprise, and what can two animals do against hundreds?» They were beaten with sticks and turned out into the cold and wet.
fact: While the Rat tells this sad part, «the unfeeling Toad broke into a snigger» — then tried to look solemn. (to snigger = to laugh in a sly, unkind way)
fact: The Wild Wooders are «lying in bed half the day, and breakfast at all hours», eating Toad's food and singing «horrid personal songs, with no humour in them» about prisons, magistrates and policemen; they say they have come to stay for good.
fact: At his front gate «a long yellow ferret with a gun» says «Who comes there?»; Toad answers crossly «Stuff and nonsense!», then drops flat in the road and «a bullet whistled over his head»; he is not hurt.
fact: The Rat: «They've got sentries posted, and they are all armed. You must just wait.» (sentries = guards on watch)
fact: Toad then rows the Rat's boat to the boat-house creek; two stoats on the bridge drop a great stone through the bottom of the boat, and it sinks.
fact: The stoats call «It will be your head next time, Toady!» and laugh «till they nearly had two fits—that is, one fit each, of course» (a fit here = a sudden attack of laughing).
fact: The Rat, calling Toad «of all the trying animals» (trying = making others tired and cross by what he does), says: «Lost me my boat that I was so fond of... And simply ruined that nice suit of clothes that I lent you!»
fact: Toad apologises and says he «will be humble and submissive, and will take no action without your kind advice and full approval» (submissive = obedient).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «gutted» (UK) = very disappointed; «sus» = suspicious; «dreich» (Scotland) = dull, wet and gloomy weather; «heaps» (Australia, NZ) = lots; «y'all» (US South) = you all.
exercise: What did the river-bankers say about Toad being sent to prison? | That he had been treated very unfairly | That it served him right | That he should stay there for ever | Nothing, because they never talked about Toad
exercise: What did the Wild Wood animals say? | That it served him right and he would never come back | That he was a hero | That they missed him | That they would visit him in prison and bring him toast
exercise: Who believed Toad would come back somehow? | Mole and Badger | The Wild Wood weasels | The magistrates and the Clerk of the court | The angry barge-woman
exercise: «Cheek and plausibility such as yours, combined with the power of a long purse.» What is «a long purse»? | Lots of money | A purse with a long strap | A long prison sentence | A stolen handbag that belonged to a washerwoman
exercise: Why did Mole and Badger move into Toad Hall? | To keep it aired and ready for Toad's return | To have a long holiday in a fine house with plenty of food | Because their homes were flooded | To sell Toad's furniture to pay his fines
exercise: What was the weather like on the night of the attack? | Very dark, windy and raining cats and dogs | Bright moonlight | Snowing | Hot and still, with thunder far away over the downs
exercise: «Raining simply cats and dogs.» What does this mean? | Raining very hard | Animals falling from the sky | Raining a little | The cats and dogs from the village came out to fight
exercise: Which animals came up the carriage-drive to the front door? | Weasels | Ferrets | Stoats | Rabbits, who had joined the Wild Wooders that night
exercise: Which animals took the backyard through the kitchen-garden? | Ferrets | Weasels | Badgers | Foxes
exercise: Where did the stoats go? | Into the conservatory and billiard-room | Up the chimney and onto the roof, to keep watch over the river | Into the boat-house, to take all Toad's boats | Into the cellar
exercise: Where were Mole and Badger when the attack came? | Sitting by the fire in the smoking-room, telling stories | Asleep upstairs in Toad's bedroom | Out on patrol in the Wild Wood, looking for Toad | In the boat-house
exercise: Why couldn't Mole and Badger win? | They were unarmed, surprised, and two against hundreds | They were fast asleep in their beds | They were afraid | Badger had hurt his leg in the snow last winter
exercise: What did Toad do while the Rat told the saddest part? | Broke into a snigger | Cried into his handkerchief | Fell fast asleep in his chair | Ran out of the room to fetch his stick and fight
exercise: Which of these are the Wild Wooders NOT doing in Toad Hall? | Keeping it clean and tidy | Lying in bed half the day | Eating Toad's food | Singing rude songs about him
exercise: What did Toad do on hearing they meant to stay for good? | Grabbed a stick and marched off | Cried into a cushion for an hour | Went straight up to bed without a word | Wrote a long, angry letter to the Chief Weasel
exercise: Who popped up behind the palings at Toad's gate? | A long yellow ferret with a gun | Two stoats with a stone | The Chief Weasel himself | A policeman, who had been waiting for Toad since the escape
exercise: «Who comes there?» What did Toad answer? | «Stuff and nonsense!» | «Only a poor washerwoman» | «A friend of the family» | «Mr. Toad of Toad Hall, the owner, and I want my house back at once!»
exercise: What did Toad do when the ferret raised the gun? | Dropped flat in the road | Ran straight at the ferret | Raised his hands to surrender | Hid behind a large oak tree in the field
exercise: What did Toad hear as he ran away? | The ferret laughing, and other thin little laughs | Bells ringing | Dogs barking | The ferret calling him back politely to talk it over
exercise: How did Toad try to reach Toad Hall the second time? | By rowing the Rat's boat up the river | By the secret passage | In a motor-car | By walking through the Wild Wood at night
exercise: What happened under the little bridge? | A great stone smashed through the bottom of the boat | The boat hit the bank | A ferret jumped down into the boat and stole the oars | The boat got stuck in the weeds
exercise: «It will be your head next time, Toady!» Who shouted this? | Two stoats on the bridge | The ferret sentry at the gate | The Chief Weasel on the lawn | The weasels in the dining-hall
exercise: What two things of the Rat's did Toad spoil? | His boat and the suit he had lent Toad | His pistols and the poems he had been writing all summer | His house and his garden | His luncheon-basket and his oars
exercise: What did Toad promise after apologising? | To take no action without the Rat's advice and approval | To buy the Rat a brand-new motor-boat with brass fittings | To go back to prison | To fight the stoats alone tomorrow
exercise: What did the Rat say they must do first? | Wait to see Mole and Badger and hear their news | Call the police | Write to the magistrates and ask them to send soldiers | Attack at once
test: Zara, gutted (very disappointed): «Mole and Badger just ran off and left Toad Hall to the weasels.» Put it right. | They fought, but two unarmed animals were beaten by hundreds | Right, they ran at the first sight of a stoat | They handed the keys to the weasels and walked away | They sold the Hall
test: Why did Toad snigger at the story of his friends being beaten and turned out? | He was thoughtless and unfeeling — not a kind reaction | He thought the whole story was a joke the Rat had made up | He was glad they were gone | He did not hear it
test: What is the sentries' plan meant to do? | Keep anyone from getting back into Toad Hall | Help Toad find his way home safely after prison | Wash the clothes | Catch fish in the river for the weasels' great banquet
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Toad was badly hurt at his gate.» | False: he dropped flat and the shot whistled over his head | True: he was shot in the leg and limped all the way home | True: the ferret bit him | False: he never went to the gate
test: Tyler: «the stoats nearly had two fits laughing, so like, 4 fits total?» Explain the joke. | «Two fits—that is, one fit each»: two stoats, one fit each, makes two | Each stoat had two fits, so the Rat is counting them carefully | It was four fits | Fits means clothes
test: What would have happened if Toad had listened to the Rat the first time? | He would have kept the Rat's boat and suit safe | He would have won Toad Hall back by himself that very evening | He would have gone to prison | He would have drowned
test: Why does the Rat call Toad «of all the trying animals»? | Toad keeps rushing off, ignoring advice, and wrecking things | Toad tries very hard at everything he does | Toad is the best animal | Toad makes nice tea
test: Ella, «it's dreich (gloomy) out»: «why attack on a wet stormy night?» What does the story suggest? | Nobody expected an attack on such a night | Weasels love rain, because it washes their fur clean | The stoats were bored | The police were away
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat say about weasels and stoats? | «They break out sometimes... you can't really trust them» | «They are the best of fellows, always kind to river-bankers» | «They never leave the Wild Wood» | «They are afraid of toads»
test: Looking back: in chapter III, who were the faces in the holes and the whistling in the Wild Wood? | The Wild Wood animals, hunting the frightened Mole | Otters | Field-mice carol singers, practising for Christmas | Rabbits playing games
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, Badger said «Any friend of mine walks where he likes in this country». Why is Toad Hall now different? | The Wild Wooders have dared to take it, Badger or no Badger | Badger has moved away to live by the sea for good | Badger made friends with the weasels | Toad Hall is not in this country
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, after Toad escaped, why did Badger say they should go on sleeping at Toad Hall? | Toad might be brought back «between two policemen» | To eat Toad's food | To guard the new red motor-car from thieves | Because it was warmer than Badger's house
test: Looking back: in chapter I, how was the Rat's boat painted? | Blue outside and white inside | Bright red, with yellow wheels and a green stripe | Canary-yellow and green, like the cart | Black, with gold lines along the side
test: Looking back: in chapter II, after the motor-car wrecked the caravan, the Rat shouted «I'll have the law of you!» How is the Rat different now? | He stays calm and says they must wait | He shouts even louder and fires his pistols in the air | He fires his pistols | He goes to the police to complain about the weasels
test: What has Toad learnt by the end of this part, at least for now? | That rushing off alone makes things worse | That stoats are friendly | That boats are far too dangerous for toads | That he should go back to prison
test: ★★★ The attack came from three sides. Match: front drive, kitchen-garden, conservatory. | Weasels, ferrets, stoats | Stoats, weasels, ferrets | Ferrets, stoats, weasels | Weasels, stoats, ferrets
test: ★★★ «What can two animals do against hundreds?» If there were 200 attackers, how many attackers were there for each defender? | 100 | 200 | 2 | 50
test: ★★★ Toad promised: «I will be humble and submissive». Which word in his promise means «obedient»? | Submissive | Humble | Kindly | Approval, which means «saying yes to a plan»
exam: Identify the conclusion which Mole and Badger drew, «arguing from history», respecting Toad's return. | That no criminal law had prevailed against cheek and plausibility combined with a long purse | That Toad would be released for good behaviour | That Toad would never return | That the magistrates would pardon him upon the payment of a fine
exam: Ascertain the meteorological conditions obtaining upon the night of the attack upon Toad Hall. | Very dark, blowing hard, and raining heavily | Clear and moonlit | Frosty, with snow lying deep upon the ground | Hot and oppressive with thunder
exam: It is given that a stone was dropped upon Toad's vessel. Select the response which most accurately identifies the perpetrators. | Two stoats upon the bridge | The ferret sentry at the gate | The Chief Weasel | A body of desperate ferrets in the kitchen-garden
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the Rat's counsel at the close of this part? | That nothing could be done until the Mole and the Badger had been consulted | That Toad should storm the Hall alone | That the police be summoned forthwith to recover the property | That Toad should enlist as a soldier
